# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_port_create(uint32_t options, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_port_bind(vx_handle port, vx_handle source,
                       enum vx_trigger trigger, uint64_t key,
                       uint64_t threshold);
int64_t vx_port_wait(vx_handle port, vx_instant deadline, vx_duration leeway,
                     vx_packet *out, size_t out_len);
vx_status vx_port_post(vx_handle port, const vx_packet *packet);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A port is a queue of packets, and the one place a thread waits for anything that takes long (the blueprint's rule 4, 00; the kernel's design, 01 §4.4).
Every event source a program cares about is bound to a port, and the program makes one call, `vx_port_wait`, to wait on all of them.
There is no timer object: the deadline is an argument of the wait.
Locks wait on a futex instead; see futex(2).

A packet is 32 bytes:

```c
typedef struct vx_packet {
  uint64_t key;   // chosen by whoever bound or posted it
  uint64_t value; // counter value, IRQ count, exit string's length;
                  // free for user posts
  vx_instant timestamp;
  uint32_t source;  // the handle it came from, or 0 for port_post
  uint32_t trigger; // enum vx_trigger
} vx_packet;
```

<timestamp> is the time the packet was made, on the one monotonic clock.

`vx_port_create` makes a port and stores a handle to it in <*out>.
<options> must be 0.
The handle carries every right except `VX_RIGHT_EXEC`, `VX_RIGHT_MAP` and `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`.

`vx_port_bind` attaches a one-shot binding to <source>: when <trigger> holds on it, the port gets one packet with <key>, and the binding is gone.
If the trigger already holds when the call is made, the packet is queued at once, so no event is lost between looking at a source and binding it; a program re-binds after handling each packet.
<port> needs `VX_RIGHT_WRITE` and <source> `VX_RIGHT_WAIT`.
The packet's <source> is the handle value given here, and its <value> depends on the trigger:

| Source | Trigger | Fires when | value |
| channel end | `VX_TRIGGER_READABLE` | a message is queued to read | messages queued |
| channel end | `VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` | the other end is gone | 0 |
| counter | `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` | the counter reaches <threshold> | the counter |
| ring end | `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` | its doorbell reaches <threshold> | the doorbell |
| ring end | `VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` | the other end is gone | 0 |
| task | `VX_TRIGGER_EXIT` | the task has ended | its exit string's length |
| Irq | `VX_TRIGGER_IRQ` | the line fired since it was last bound | times fired in all |
| DmaDomain | `VX_TRIGGER_DMA_FAULT` | its faults exceed <threshold> | faults in all |

<threshold> is used only by `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` and `VX_TRIGGER_DMA_FAULT`.
An `EXIT` packet's value is 0 when the task succeeded; the exit string itself comes from `task_info`.
Each binding is the packet it will deliver, allocated when it is bound, so bindings never overflow a port.
A binding whose source is destroyed before it fires goes with it, delivering nothing.
Two triggers are not bound here: `VX_TRIGGER_EXCEPTION` comes from `exception_bind` and `VX_TRIGGER_PAGER` from `pager_create`.

`vx_port_wait` stores up to <out_len> packets in <out> and returns how many, at least one.
Packets from fired bindings come first, oldest first, then posted ones in the order they were posted.
If none is queued it blocks until one is, or until <deadline> passes; <leeway> lets the kernel wake it up to that many nanoseconds late, so one timer interrupt can serve several waits.
A <deadline> of `VX_INFINITE` never comes, and a <deadline> already past makes the call a poll.
<port> needs `VX_RIGHT_WAIT`.
<out_len> is 1 to 64.
Several threads may wait on one port; each packet wakes one of them, the longest waiting first.

`vx_port_post` queues a copy of <*packet>, for self-wakes and user events.
The kernel sets its <timestamp> to now, its <source> to 0 and its <trigger> to `VX_TRIGGER_USER`; <key> and <value> are the caller's.
<port> needs `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`.
Posts wait in a ring of 64 per port.

A port's packets that nobody takes are freed with the port when its last handle is closed.

`port_cancel` is reserved.
The design names it in the syscall surface (01 §3) without describing it; it has no wrapper, and `vx_syscall(VX_SYS_port_cancel, ...)` returns `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` today.

# EXAMPLES

A binding fires once, and at once if its condition already holds; from the kernel's tests:

```c
  CHECK(vx_port_bind(port, ch[1], VX_TRIGGER_READABLE, 11, 0) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(1), 0, pk, 4) == VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT);
  note out = {};
  CHECK(vx_channel_write(ch[0], &out, sizeof out, nullptr, 0) == VX_OK);
  CHECK(vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(100), 0, pk, 4) == 1);
```

Waiting for a child task to end:

```c
  if (vx_port_bind(port, task, VX_TRIGGER_EXIT, 99, 0) != VX_OK) return nullptr;
  if (vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(2000), 0, &pk, 1) != 1 || pk.key != 99)
    return nullptr;
```

